      "text": "To find the probability that both chosen members are girls, we can use the formula for the probability of two independent events occurring:\n\nP(A and B) = P(A) \\* P(B|A)\n\nHere, A represents the event that the first member chosen is a girl, and B represents the event that the second member chosen is also a girl.\n\nSince there are 5 girls out of a total of 10 members, the probability of selecting a girl on the first choice is 5/10 or 1/2.\n\nAfter one member has been chosen, there are now 4 remaining girls and a total of 9 members left. Therefore, the probability of selecting another girl is 4/9.\n\nTo find the combined probability, we multiply the individual probabilities:\n\nP(Both Girls) = P(A) \\* P(B|A) = (1/2) \\* (4/9) = 4/18\n\nTo simplify the fraction, we can divide both the numerator and the denominator by 2:\n\nP(Both Girls) = 2/9\n\nSo, the probability that both chosen members are girls is 2/9.",
